    {
      "slug": "alberta-bc-yukon",
      "title": "The Canadian West",
      "date": "May 2024, May 2026, July 2026",
      "subtitle": "",
      "blurb": "Alberta, British Columbia, Yukon. Canada’s west. To me, they have always been afterthoughts. Stops, not destinations. In May 2024 I, together with a friend, drove from California to Alaska, passing through Vancouver and stretches of the Alaska Highway in BC and Yukon. On the way back by myself, it was Yukon – BC – Alberta, visiting the famed Jasper and Banff National Parks for the first time.\n\nMay 2026, the expedition to Alaska was on again and the passage through Canada’s West was repeated, but with the order reversed. The way there was Alberta – BC – Yukon via the national parks and then again along the Alaska Highway. The way back was Yukon – BC, via the Stewart Cassiar Highway.\n\nMy time spent across the three provinces/territory totals to probably less than two weeks, which includes four national parks (Jasper, Banff, Waterton Lakes, and Kluane) and two times each on the Alaska and Stewart Cassiar Highways.\n\nI want to go back for a proper trip: Vancouver Island, Bella Coola, Dempster Highway, the rest of the Canadian National Parks in the Rockies… Oh, and Haida Gwaii, a kayak camping trip meandering through uninhabited islands and historical totem poles.\n\nUntil then…",
